## Frontend Runtime

The Nuxt application lives in `frontend/` and is a standalone frontend. This
profile does not include a backend, external API integration, or generated
OpenAPI client; the generated TypeScript OpenAPI client belongs to a later
full-stack profile.

Install dependencies:

```bash
make setup
```

Run the Nuxt development server with HMR:

```bash
make dev
```

Build the production Nuxt output:

```bash
make build
```

`make build` writes the Nuxt server output to `frontend/.output/`. The server
entry point is `frontend/.output/server/index.mjs`.

Run the already built application without HMR:

```bash
make run
```

By default the built server listens on `http://127.0.0.1:8000`; override with
`HOST` and `PORT`. This is a shared-environment runtime smoke path, not a
production deployment.

Quality checks:

```bash
make check
```

`make check` validates the project dashboard, runs ESLint, Nuxt typecheck, and
Vitest. The main page composes a small `welcome` feature from `frontend/features/`.
{% endif %}

{% if project_type == "fullstack" %}
## Full-Stack Local Runtime

The FastAPI backend lives in `backend/`. The Nuxt frontend lives in `frontend/`.
The frontend API client is generated from the backend OpenAPI contract; do not
edit files under `frontend/shared/api/generated/` by hand.

Install dependencies and generate the initial client:

```bash
make setup
```

OpenAPI and generated client commands:

```bash
make api-schema
make api-generate
make api-check
```

`make api-schema` loads the real FastAPI app and writes
`artifacts/openapi.json`. The schema is not committed; it is regenerated before
client generation and drift checks. `make api-generate` writes the TypeScript
SDK and types to `frontend/shared/api/generated/`. The generator is
`@hey-api/openapi-ts` because it produces typed SDK functions from OpenAPI
operation IDs without adding a Nuxt-specific runtime lock-in. `make api-check`
generates into a temporary directory and compares it with the current generated
client.

Run development servers with reload/HMR:

```bash
make dev
```

Build both artifacts:

```bash
make build
```

`make build` writes the backend wheel and sdist to `backend/dist/` and the Nuxt
server output to `frontend/.output/`.

Run the already built backend and Nuxt server together:

```bash
make run
```

Override ports with `BACKEND_HOST`, `BACKEND_PORT`, `FRONTEND_HOST`, and
`FRONTEND_PORT`. The frontend API base URL defaults to the backend URL and can
be overridden with `NUXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL`; see `frontend/.env.example`.

Quality and browser checks:

```bash
make check
make e2e
```

`make check` validates the project dashboard, runs backend Ruff, Pyright and
pytest, verifies OpenAPI/client drift, and runs frontend ESLint, Nuxt typecheck
and Vitest. `make e2e` expects the built app to already be running and uses
Playwright against `E2E_BASE_URL`.

Current project direction is tracked in `project/index.md`.

{% if runtime_level == "production" %}
## Production-Like Runtime

This production profile creates local, deployable OCI artifacts and verifies the
runtime contract without deploying to cloud infrastructure.

End-to-end production artifact path:

```bash
make setup
make api-check
make check
make build
make image-build
make image-inspect
make prod-up
make prod-status
make prod-smoke
make e2e-production
make prod-down
```

`make image-build` builds separate backend and frontend images. The backend
runtime image runs Uvicorn without reload as a non-root user. The frontend
runtime image runs the Nuxt server output with Node, without HMR or source
mounts. `make image-inspect` checks image existence, non-root users,
entrypoint/command, exposed ports, and absence of development runtime commands.
`make prod-up` starts both images through Compose, `make prod-status` reports
service state, health, images, and ports, `make prod-smoke` checks backend
health, readiness, OpenAPI, API data, frontend HTML, and frontend security
headers, and `make e2e-production` runs Playwright against the production-like
frontend.

The frontend production runtime sets a minimal local security header baseline:
`X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`,
`Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin`, and a CSP with
`default-src 'self'`, `object-src 'none'`, `base-uri 'self'`, and
`frame-ancestors 'none'`. The CSP permits Nuxt hydration with inline scripts
and styles. TLS and HSTS are intentionally left to the required external
ingress or reverse proxy contract.

Runtime configuration is environment-driven:

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `PROD_BACKEND_PORT` | `8000` | Host port mapped to backend container port `8000`. |
| `PROD_FRONTEND_PORT` | `3000` | Host port mapped to frontend container port `3000`. |
| `NUXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL` | `http://127.0.0.1:${PROD_BACKEND_PORT}` | Browser-visible API base URL. |
| `BACKEND_IMAGE` | `{{ project_slug }}-backend:production` | Backend image tag. |
| `FRONTEND_IMAGE` | `{{ project_slug }}-frontend:production` | Frontend image tag. |
| `COMPOSE` | `docker compose` | Compose command used by production targets. |

The production profile means reproducible local OCI images, production process
commands, health/readiness checks, graceful Compose shutdown, minimal runtime
dependencies, and CI-verifiable smoke and browser checks.

It does not mean automatic deployment, high availability, Kubernetes readiness,
database backups, unlimited scale, public registry publishing, real production
secrets, or complete future security/compliance coverage.
